{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310746,"nonce":"SUnBLBU24C+KRPCHteGEpVELJx4=","title":"wasm parallel flash attention race — exhaustively diagnosed 2026-06-17, root cause is wasmtime SharedMemory cross-instance consistency, NOT ...","message":"**RESOLVED 2026-06-17 (commit 4951aa3, branch wasm-default-inguest-matmul) — it was SHADOW-STACK OVERLAP, not malloc/TLS/memory-model.** Parallel flash now bit-identical to sequential (8/8 greedy + Paris), ON by default (RAYZOR_FLASH_PAR=0 opts out; self-gates cache_len\u003e=256). ROOT CAUSE (found via a standalone wasmtime reproducer): the host pool gives each worker instance a private 1 MiB shadow stack (worker_main: rayzor_malloc + set __stack_pointer), but TWO bugs broke it — (1) runtime-wasm never EXPORTED __stack_pointer, so get_export returned None and the set silently fell through → every worker on the SAME default stack; (2) rayzor_malloc (guest allocator over shared mem) is NOT thread-safe, so the N workers reserving CONCURRENTLY at pool init collide on the same base → overlapping stacks even with the export. Concurrent frames corrupt each other; matmul survived (tiny pure-SIMD frame), the deeper flash frame did not. FIX: export __stack_pointer (runtime-wasm/.cargo/config) + serialize the reservation with a mutex in worker_main. THE MEMORY-MODEL ANGLE WAS A RED HERRING — host-mutex join + disjoint writes is deterministic once stacks don't overlap (reproducer: reserved stack 0/300, shared stack 300/300). Perf ~5-11% decode at cache~280, grows with context, more stable. Kept a guest SC atomic gate (FLASH_DONE) as correct discipline. The standalone reproducer (the decisive experiment) is what cracked it after every in-context fix failed.\n\n---\n\nIn-guest parallel flash attention (q-head banding across the worker pool) WAS non-deterministic at cache_len\u003e=256; the prior revert (8b90d1b) blamed \"worker malloc / lack of TLS\" — close (it IS the allocator + the stack) but the precise mechanism is the shadow-stack reservation above, not in-kernel malloc. Investigation trail below kept for the methodology lessons.\n\n**Methodology lesson FIRST:** measuring divergence via the generated TEXT (md5) is probabilistic + amplified. Pass-isolation diagnostics gave clean 4/4 streaks that were LUCK against a ~50-60% race — they misled me into a false \"scalar-store fixes it\" conclusion. Verify with GREEDY (temp 0) — sequential is then bit-stable (rules out sampling/temperature), and small samples lie. User questions that forced rigor: \"checking 1:1 or inferring accuracy?\", \"considered temperature variance?\", \"dynamic components linking?\" — all good.\n\n**RULED OUT (each tested, not assumed):**\n- worker malloc / TLS — made fully alloc-free (per-band scores pre-allocated in caller); still races.\n- memory ordering — paired SeqCst release (worker end) + acquire (after join) fences DON'T help.\n- timing / workers-still-running — a 5,000,000-iter post-join busy-wait does NOT help.\n- the join — a shared `AtomicU32` counter (in linear memory, shared across instances → atomics DO work cross-instance) shows worker_runs_delta=7=expected EVERY call, all incremented BEFORE join returns. Join genuinely waits.\n- disjoint-write overlap — bands are disjoint q-head rows of out_data; scratch is disjoint per band.\n- SIMD vs scalar store — in-build A/B (RAYZOR_FLASH_SIMD_STORE): both diverge 6/6.\n- dynamic-link / fn_idx (USER'S LEAD) — runtime DOES dynamic-link (host wasm-linker merges runtime table=50/60 + user=1110 → 1727 funcs, 158 imports resolved/252 stubbed, GOT.func 24/24 unresolved→0). BUT flash_fn_idx is valid + distinct from matmul_fn_idx, and worker_runs_delta=7 proves the correct function dispatches + runs. NOT the cause.\n- temperature — greedy temp=0 parallel still diverges (3 distinct/4) while sequential is 3/3 bit-stable.\n\n**KEY CLUE → ROOT CAUSE:** a SEQUENTIAL RECOMPUTE on the main thread after join reliably makes out_data correct (0 divergences / 6 via an in-process diff), but a pure busy-wait does NOT, and fences do NOT. So only main-thread MEMORY TRAFFIC reading the shared region refreshes THIS instance's view of the workers' non-atomic writes. Signature of a **wasmtime SharedMemory cross-instance consistency issue**: each worker is a separate wasmtime Store/instance sharing one linear memory (build_worker_pool); the atomic counter is visible but the workers' non-atomic f32 stores to out_data are not reliably visible to the main instance right after join. The MATMUL survives only because its output is consumed later/differently (not immediately after its join). This is BELOW the Haxe/runtime-wasm layer.\n\n**RESEARCH + GUEST-ATOMIC FIX ATTEMPT (2026-06-17, workflow wf_6ebfccc9):** 7-agent web research confirmed the ESTABLISHED real-world pattern: cross-thread visibility needs a paired atomic on the SAME shared location in the SAME memory model. Native pools get it from thread::join (synchronizes-with). wasm threading (Emscripten/wasi-threads/wasm-bindgen-rayon) REQUIRES a GUEST wasm atomic — wasi-libc pthread_join writes result non-atomically then atomic-stores detach_state + notify; joiner atomic.wait + re-reads → publishes the non-atomic result. wasm has only SC atomics (no acq/rel). A host Mutex/Condvar join is OUT of the wasm model. Adversary CORRECTED the synthesis: Cranelift is NOT hoisting (Opcode::Call clears its alias table per inst_predicates.rs has_memory_fence_semantics), so the cause was claimed to be hardware coherence. IMPLEMENTED the textbook fix: static AtomicU32 FLASH_DONE; worker fetch_add(SeqCst) AFTER band writes; main SC-load-spins it before returning. Disassembly CONFIRMS real i32.atomic.rmw.add/load emit (not stubbed). **RESULT: did NOT fix it — 8/8 greedy runs still divergent (3 distinct).** Per the adversary's own discriminator this REFUTES the pure memory-model explanation: the HB edge is now correctly established yet out_data is still non-deterministic → the corruption is in concurrent COMPUTATION, not cross-thread visibility. (The atomic gate is kept anyway — correct discipline; matmul is only luck-correct.) The recompute \"fix\" (0/6) was a RED HERRING — it OVERWRITES out_data with the sequential result, so it proves nothing about visibility.\n\n**WHAT'S STILL UNEXPLAINED:** workers run the correct fn (delta=7), compute a deterministic pure fn over DISJOINT inputs/scratch/output, with a now-correct guest-atomic HB edge — yet produce non-deterministic out_data. No shared mutable state found in flash_attn_decode_one_qhead / dot_slice_f32 / axpy_slice. Suspects NOT yet ruled out: (a) worker wasm INSTANCE state — each worker is a separate Store/instance sharing linear mem but with its OWN globals incl. __stack_pointer (1MiB stack reserved via rayzor_malloc at pool init, wasm_runner.rs:1180); if those stack reservations RACE/overlap at lazy pool init, deep flash frames corrupt while shallow matmul frames don't. (b) the SharedMemory::data() UnsafeCell UB contract (wasmtime docs: \"safe access not possible through normal loads/stores\"). (c) Cranelift miscompiling the flash kernel under the threaded/shared config.\n\n**DECISIVE NEXT EXPERIMENT (adversary-specified, NOT yet done):** minimal STANDALONE wasm32-wasip1-threads module + tiny wasmtime harness reproducing the EXACT topology (N worker Stores sharing one SharedMemory, host mpsc dispatch + host mutex join). producer(band) writes a known pattern into a disjoint band; consumer checksums after join. Run 1000x with/without the guest atomic. This isolates wasmtime/topology from all nue/flash confounds — the ONLY way to tell (a)/(b)/(c) apart. Sources in the workflow output: webassembly.github.io/threads/core/exec/relaxed.html, wasi-libc musl pthread_join, crossbeam-deque, wasmtime SharedMemory docs, Cranelift inst_predicates.rs/alias_analysis.rs. See [[bugs_wasm_oom_and_object_freeing]], [[perf_wasm_inguest_worker_pool]], [[bugs_wasm_linker_silent_unreachable]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_flash_parallel_sharedmem_race` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}